::GEO_U03_L04_GP_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L04_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.1
Question:
Which criterion uses three pairs of corresponding sides?
{
=A. SSS#SSS stands for side-side-side, three corresponding side pairs.
~B. SAS#SAS uses two sides and the included angle.
~C. ASA#ASA uses two angles and the included side.
~D. HL#HL applies only to right triangles.
}

::GEO_U03_L04_GP_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L04_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.1
Question:
Which criterion uses two sides and the included angle?
{
~A. SSS#SSS has three side pairs.
=B. SAS#SAS uses two corresponding sides and the angle between them.
~C. AAS#AAS uses two angles and a non-included side.
~D. AAA#AAA does not prove congruence.
}

::GEO_U03_L04_GP_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L04_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.1
Question:
Which criterion uses two angles and the included side?
{
~A. AAS#AAS uses a non-included side.
~B. SAS#SAS uses two sides and the included angle.
=C. ASA#ASA uses two angles and the side between them.
~D. SSA#SSA is not a valid congruence criterion in this lesson.
}

::GEO_U03_L04_GP_Q04::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L04_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.1
Question:
Which criterion uses two angles and a non-included side?
{
~A. ASA#ASA requires the side between the two angles.
~B. SSS#SSS uses three side pairs.
~C. HL#HL is for right triangles.
=D. AAS#AAS uses two angles and a side not between them.
}

::GEO_U03_L04_GP_Q05::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L04_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.TRI.3
Question:
Why can valid triangle congruence criteria be connected to rigid transformations?
{
~A. They change side lengths until two triangles match.#Rigid transformations do not change side lengths. They preserve distances while mapping one figure onto another.
~B. They rely only on the triangles looking the same.#Appearance is not proof. Congruence criteria give mathematical evidence that supports a rigid-motion match.
~C. They prove only that corresponding angles are equal.#Some angle information may be involved, but triangle congruence also requires enough evidence to fix size and shape.
=D. They provide enough evidence to map one triangle exactly onto the other.#Correct. Valid congruence criteria give enough side and angle information to justify that a rigid transformation can map one triangle exactly onto the other.
}

